<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="L-serine biosynthesis II" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-8011" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: SER| biosynthesis is a major metabolic pathway in many organisms. Its end product, |FRAME: SER|, is not only used in protein synthesis, but also as a precursor for the biosynthesis of glycine, cysteine, tryptophan, and phospholipids. In addition, it directly or indirectly serves as a source of one-carbon units for the biosynthesis of various compounds. Three different |FRAME: SER| biosynthetic pathways operate in plants. The best studied pathway occurs in relation to |FRAME: PWY-181 photorespiration|, where serine is formed from two molecules of |FRAME: GLY|. Another pathway, which operates in plastids and forms |FRAME: 3-P-HYDROXYPYRUVATE| as an intermediate, is described in |FRAME: SERSYN-PWY|. The third pathway, described here, is initiated in the cytosol and involves a |FRAME: GLYCERATE| intermediate. &lt;b&gt;Sbout This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; The glycerate&amp;ndash;serine pathway is initiated in the cytosol by the enzyme |FRAME: EC-3.1.3.38| (PGA phosphatase). This enzyme, which has been described in 1971 |CITS: [10970181]|, has not been studied since and the gene encoding it is not known yet. The enzyme has a broad specificity, but |FRAME: G3P| is its preferred substrate. It is confined to the cytosol and is active at low pH (optimum pH 5.9). Its activity was found high in a C4 plant (sorghum), where serine formation via the photorespiratory pathway is very limited due to a low or absent photorespiration. So far PGA phosphatase has only been reported from plants. The second step of the pathway is catalyzed by isoforms of glycerate dehydrogenase/hydroxypyruvate reductases (HPRs). Three isoforms of hydroxypyruvate reductase have been described in plants - a peroxisomal form (|FRAME: AT1G68010|) |CITS: [9177031]|, a cytosolic form (|FRAME: AT1G79870|) |CITS: [3548703][3281657][16667010][2039466][18952776]|, and a plastidal form (|FRAME: AT1G12550|) |CITS: [16666441][21205613]|. While the cytosolic isoform is the most relevant to this cytosolic pathway, it is not impossible that the pathway occurs under certain conditions also in the peroxisome or plastid |CITS: [29593770]|. Enzymes catalyzing the last step of the pathway, aminotransferases that form |FRAME: SER| from |FRAME: OH-PYR|, exist in the peroxisome, cytosol and plastid |CITS: [21205613]|. The reaction can proceed with either |FRAME: L-ALPHA-ALANINE| (|FRAME: EC-2.6.1.51|) or |FRAME: GLY| (|FRAME: EC-2.6.1.45|) as the amino group donor. The Arabidopsis enzyme |FRAME: AGT1|, which is classified as |FRAME: EC-2.6.1.14|, can transaminate |FRAME: OH-PYR| using |FRAME: L-ALPHA-ALANINE|, |FRAME: ASN|, |FRAME: GLY| and other amino acids |CITS: [23098902]|. While the first enzyme of the pathway has so far been described only from plants, the later steps of the pathway, resulting in the production of |FRAME: SER| from |FRAME: GLYCERATE|, have been described from animals |CITS: [13385257]| and bacteria |CITS: [4315933]|.</Description>
